  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "A "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "triple-treble"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "triple treble"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " lap blanket is 80 x 90 cm. The very tall stitches mean the blanket needs only about 27 rows to reach 90 cm in chunky yarn. The gaps between stitches are large and give the fabric a dramatic open look."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Chunky yarn keeps the open stitch from becoming too flimsy. A fine yarn would produce a lacework-like fabric; chunky gives enough body for a lap blanket. Total yardage is around 350 g because the tall open stitch uses less yarn than a dense fabric."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "suppliesCard",
        "attrs": {
          "heading": "Materials",
          "items": [
            {
              "name": "Chunky yarn",
              "qty": "350 g"
            },
            {
              "name": "8 mm crochet hook",
              "qty": "1"
            },
            {
              "name": "Tapestry needle",
              "qty": "1"
            },
            {
              "name": "Scissors",
              "qty": "1 pair"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "How to work a triple treble"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Yarn over 3 times. Insert hook into stitch. Pull up loop (5 loops on hook). *Yarn over, draw through 2 loops*. Repeat 4 times. One trtr made."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Gauge"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "7 trtr x 3 rows = 10 x 10 cm in chunky with an 8 mm hook. Each row gains a lot of height; check after 5 rows to confirm the pace."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitches used"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "ch: chain"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "trtr (UK) / tr (US): triple treble"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Foundation chain"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Chain 57 (56 trtr + 1 edge)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Row 1"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "turning-chain"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "Turning chain"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " of ch 5. Work 1 trtr into every chain from the 6th. Turn. (56 trtr.)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Row 2 and onward"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Ch 5. Skip the first stitch. Work 1 trtr into every stitch. Work 1 trtr into the top of the turning chain. Turn."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Repeat Row 2 until piece measures 90 cm (approximately 27 rows). Fasten off."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Edging"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Work 2 rounds of dc (UK) around the perimeter. Work 1 dc into each trtr along top and bottom. Work 3 dc into each row-end gap on the sides. Work 3 dc into each corner. The edging closes the very open side edges."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Finishing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Secure both tails with "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "weaving-in-ends"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "weaving in ends"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". Block lightly to open the lattice."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Care"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Handwash wool cool. Dry flat. Acrylic: machine wash 40 degrees, tumble dry low."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What to try next"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The double treble baby blanket uses a slightly shorter stitch in DK for a more controlled open fabric. Or try the chunky throw for a dense dc version in the same yarn weight."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
